    <string-array name="help_bodies">
        <item>1) Open BTC Wallet and tap Create — write the 12 words on paper. 2) Show the QR code to receive money. 3) Try Secure Message with a friend: one shows the QR, the other scans it, then chat.</item>
        <item>The wallet lives on your phone — keys never leave it. The 12 words ARE your money: whoever has them can spend it. Write them on paper, never photograph them, and verify you wrote them correctly before sending money in.</item>
        <item>Paste the address or scan the QR of who pays you, enter the amount, pick a fee (Slow = cheaper, Fast = quicker), check the summary, confirm. The fee goes to the Bitcoin network, not to v01d.</item>
        <item>Create or import a 25-word seed, then show the address to receive Monero. For balance and sending, v01d hands off to Monerujo, a dedicated Monero app — your same seed works there.</item>
        <item>Messages are end-to-end encrypted: only you and your contact can read them, not even the relay server can. Setup: pick a display name, choose the public relay, done. Add contacts by scanning each other\'s QR in person.</item>
        <item>An alias is an alternative email that forwards to your real mailbox — sites never learn your real address. Connect a free addy.io account, type the site name, tap Create. Turn an alias off when it starts receiving spam.</item>
        <item>A quick health check of your phone\'s privacy: verified system, screen lock, VPN, DNS and more. Follow the suggestions to raise your score. Checked data stays on the phone.</item>
        <item>Tor bounces your traffic through volunteer relays so websites cannot see your IP. Flip the VPN switch, accept Android\'s prompt, and wait for it to connect. It is slower than normal — that is the price of anonymity.</item>
        <item>The PIN (or fingerprint) locks the app, not your money — seeds always work even without it. Wipe All Data in Settings deletes wallets, chats and settings permanently: use it before giving the phone away.</item>
        <item>Seeds on paper, never digital. Verify contacts in person. A fresh address per payment is more private. Practice on Testnet first. If an offer sounds too good, it is a scam — v01d will never ask for your seed.</item>
    </string-array>
